.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library operates on TrustedZFS dataset handles: file descriptors that
denote one ZFS dataset (or, with
.Dv ZHF_SUBTREE ,
a dataset and its descendants) and carry an immutable rights mask.
A handle pins its dataset by guid, so it follows renames and reports
.Er ENXIO
once the dataset is destroyed.
Handles pass over
.Xr unix 4
sockets, survive
.Xr fork 2 ,
and may be confined further with
.Xr cap_rights_limit 2
and per-command
.Xr cap_ioctls_limit 2 .
.Pp
.Fn tzfs_open
mints a handle by dataset name via
.Pa /dev/zfs
and is the only name-based call; minting requires the corresponding
.Xr zfs-allow 8
delegations (or root) and, in a jail, dataset visibility.
All other calls operate purely through descriptors and work inside
.Xr capsicum 4
capability mode, with the exception of
.Fn tzfs_blkopen .
.Pp
The rights bits are
.Dv ZH_PROPS_READ , ZH_PROPS_WRITE , ZH_SNAPSHOT , ZH_SNAP_DESTROY ,
.Dv ZH_ROLLBACK , ZH_CLONE_SRC , ZH_CREATE , ZH_DESTROY , ZH_SEND ,
.Dv ZH_RECV , ZH_MOUNT , ZH_HOLD , ZH_RENAME , ZH_PROMOTE ,
.Dv ZH_BOOKMARK ,
and
.Dv ZH_RELEASE ;
.Dv ZH_PROPS_READ
and
.Dv ZH_EVENT
are implicit on every handle.
.Fn tzfs_derive
returns a new handle for the same dataset with a subset of the rights;
requesting rights not held fails with
.Er ENOTCAPABLE .
.Fn tzfs_openat
opens a descendant dataset (or
.Dq @snapshot
of the handle itself) with a subset of the rights; descending requires a
.Dv ZHF_SUBTREE
handle and relative names may not escape the grant.
.Ss Capsicum ioctl confinement
.Fn tzfs_limit_dataset_ioctls
and
.Fn tzfs_limit_pool_ioctls
apply a typed, exact
.Xr cap_ioctls_limit 2
allowlist to one descriptor.
The
.Fa ops
argument is a combination of the
.Dv TZFS_OP_*
bits declared in
.In trustedzfs.h .
Dataset and pool operation bits may not be mixed; an unknown or wrong-kind
bit fails with
.Er EINVAL
without changing the descriptor.
An empty mask is valid and denies all ioctls.
As with the underlying Capsicum primitive, confinement is monotonic: a later
call may narrow the set but cannot restore a removed operation.
.Pp
.Fn tzfs_limit_dataset_ioctls_by_rights
constructs the complete dataset ioctl profile permitted by the supplied
.Dv ZH_*
rights and
.Dv ZHF_SUBTREE
scope.
It includes the implicit inspection operations, permits descendant
open and enumeration only for a subtree grant, and includes
.Dv ZFD_RENAME
only for
.Dv ZH_RENAME .
.Fn tzfs_limit_pool_ioctls_by_rights
does the corresponding mapping for pool handles.
These helpers install both a kernel operation ceiling and a per-descriptor
Capsicum ceiling; they do not replace the immutable rights enforced by the
TrustedZFS kernel handle.
.Pp
An ioctl that returns a new handle descriptor, including derive, openat,
create, clone, and pool-root-open, inherits the parent's kernel operation
ceiling even though it does not copy the Capsicum per-descriptor allowlist.
This prevents a child descriptor from escaping through a command omitted by
the grantor.
Code retaining such a result should still call the appropriate limiter to
install the matching Capsicum fast-path before using or transferring it.
An allowlist is preserved by ordinary descriptor duplication and
.Dv SCM_RIGHTS
transfer.
.Pp
.Fn tzfs_get_props
returns all properties as a packed native-encoded nvlist in a
.Xr malloc 3 Ns ed
buffer; use
.Xr nvlist_unpack 3
for structured access.
.Pp
.Fn tzfs_create
and
.Fn tzfs_create_volume
create a child filesystem or volume and return a handle for it carrying
the parent handle's rights.
.Fn tzfs_destroy
with a
.Dv NULL
.Fa relname
destroys the handle's own dataset.
.Fn tzfs_clone
creates
.Fa relname
under
.Fa zfd
(which needs
.Dv ZH_CREATE )
from the origin handle's snapshot; the origin handle needs
.Dv ZH_CLONE_SRC .
.Pp
.Fn tzfs_send
writes a replication stream for the named snapshot (or the handle itself,
if it is a snapshot handle and
.Fa snap
is
.Dv NULL )
to
.Fa out_fd ;
.Fa flags
is a combination of
.Dv ZFD_SEND_EMBED , ZFD_SEND_LARGEBLOCK , ZFD_SEND_COMPRESS ,
and
.Dv ZFD_SEND_RAW .
.Fn tzfs_recv
consumes the stream's BEGIN record from
.Fa in_fd
and receives the remainder into
.Fa reltarget .
The stream descriptors are ordinary file descriptors
.Pq pipes, sockets, files
and are checked for
.Dv CAP_WRITE
or
.Dv CAP_READ
respectively.
.Pp
.Fn tzfs_blkopen
opens the handle's volume as a block-device file descriptor suitable for
handing to a hypervisor or filesystem tools.
It currently resolves through
.Xr devfs 4
and therefore fails with
.Er ECAPMODE
in capability mode; open before
.Xr cap_enter 2 .
.Pp
.Fn tzfs_pool_open
mints a pool handle.
.Fn tzfs_pool_get_props
returns the pool property nvlist, while
.Fn tzfs_pool_set_prop_string
accepts only the string property
.Dv bootfs ;
all broader pool-property mutations fail with
.Er EPERM .
.Fn tzfs_pool_scrub
controls scrub state, and
.Fn tzfs_pool_root_open
returns a rights-limited handle for the pool root dataset.
.Pp
.Fn tzfs_list_children ,
.Fn tzfs_list_snapshots ,
.Fn tzfs_holds ,
and
.Fn tzfs_list_bookmarks
enumerate a subtree, a dataset's snapshots, a snapshot's user holds, or a
dataset's bookmarks, returning all names as a packed native-encoded nvlist
in a
.Xr malloc 3 Ns ed
buffer (iterate with
.Xr nvlist_next_nvpair 3 ) .
One cursor-free enumeration is limited to
.Dv ZFSHANDLE_ENUM_MAX_ENTRIES ;
larger sets fail with
.Er E2BIG .
The read-only boot tunable
.Va vfs.zfs.trustedzfs.enum_max_entries
may lower, but cannot raise, that production maximum.
.Fn tzfs_list_children
requires a
.Dv ZHF_SUBTREE
handle.
.Fn tzfs_get_one_prop
reads a single property (native properties resolve even at their default
value);
.Fn tzfs_inherit
clears a property back to its inherited or received source.
.Fn tzfs_promote
promotes a clone above its origin snapshot.
.Fn tzfs_bookmark
and
.Fn tzfs_destroy_bookmark
manage bookmarks, the persistent incremental-send anchors.
The send-once handle flags are fixed at mint time and enforced in shared
kernel lineage state, so deriving, opening a child, duplicating, forking, or
passing the descriptor cannot reset them.
.Dv ZHF_SEND_ONCE
makes every handle in the lineage refuse further sends after one successful
stream
.Pq Er EALREADY ,
and
.Dv ZHF_SEND_CONSUME
additionally invalidates the entire lineage.
A failed send does not consume the one attempt.
The output descriptor is never closed by the kernel; that remains the
caller's responsibility.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Functions documented as returning a handle or file descriptor return a
non-negative descriptor on success.
All others return 0 on success.
On error, \-1 is returned and
.Va errno
is set.
Operations on a handle whose dataset has been destroyed, or whose pool is
exported, fail with
.Er ENXIO ;
kevent
.Dv EVFILT_READ
on the handle reports invalidation.
.Pp
Every descriptor returned by this interface has
.Dv FD_CLOEXEC
set.
TrustedZFS is available only to 64-bit kernels and 64-bit user ABIs.
.Sh SEE ALSO
